--TEST--
`Core\Session::start` in a program answering no request throws a `LogicError` naming the absent request, and it asks that question before it asks anything about the store — `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`, `rule:core-api/session-roster`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A store *is* chosen here, and that is the point of the file. The refusal below
# has to be the one about the absent request, and a tree that configured nothing
# cannot tell that refusal apart from
# `rule:http-server/session-backend-is-shared-or-db-and-local-is-refused-at-boot`'s
# — both would stop `start()` and only the message would say which. Nothing ever
# reaches the store: `start` asks whether a request arrived first
# (`crates/nvs-stdlib/src/session.rs:752`).
[session]
backend = "shared"
--FILE--
<?nvs
// This case writes none of the seven request sections, so it is a program
// answering no request — `crates/nvs-test/src/lib.rs`'s module doc is where that
// is decided. `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate` reaches one step
// wider than an isolate for exactly this shape: a CLI program, a scheduled
// script, a job worker and a test all run with nothing inbound, and a session
// belongs to the client a request came from, so there is none to open.
uint $refused = 0;
string $said = "";
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (LogicError $noRequest) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    $said = $noRequest->message;
}
echo "start was refused ", $refused, " of 1\n";

// The class is the assertion above; the sentence is the one below. An empty
// session would say the request arrived and carried no session, which is a
// different fact from no request arriving at all, and the message is how the
// program is told which of the two it is looking at.
echo "and it says there is no request here: ",
    Core\Str::contains($said, "there is no request here") ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo "and it names the member the program wrote: ",
    Core\Str::contains($said, "Core\\Session::start()") ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The bound on the other side of the ordering. `[session] backend` is written in
// this tree, so a `start` that asked the store question first would have opened a
// record here and minted an identifier for a caller that can never present one.
// A refusal naming the directive would mean the questions run the other way
// round, so the absence of that name is what is asserted.
echo "and it is not the store's refusal: ",
    Core\Str::contains($said, "[session]") ? "no" : "yes", "\n";

// Agreement across the classes the rule governs: `Core\Request` owes the same
// answer for the same reason, so the two refuse alike rather than each growing
// its own story about a context with nothing inbound.
uint $alike = 0;
try {
    var $verb = Core\Request::method();
} catch (LogicError $noRequestEither) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($noRequestEither->message, "there is no request here")) {
        $alike = $alike + 1;
    }
}
try {
    var $page = Core\Request::query("page");
} catch (LogicError $stillNone) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($stillNone->message, "there is no request here")) {
        $alike = $alike + 1;
    }
}
echo "Core\\Request refuses alike ", $alike, " of 2\n";

// And the exemption, which is the half that keeps this a rule about requests
// rather than a rule about `Core`. Whether a shutdown has begun is a fact about
// the process, so `Core\Server::isDraining` answers here as it answers in a
// child isolate — `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/server.rs:25-28` decided what a program
// nobody is serving reads.
echo "the process is still readable: draining=",
    Core\Server::isDraining() ? "true" : "false", "\n";

// It is catchable at the root of spec § 10's tree as well: a program that cannot
// open a session is one that can say so, not one that dies.
uint $byRoot = 0;
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (Throwable $any) {
    $byRoot = $byRoot + 1;
}
echo "caught by the root of the tree ", $byRoot, " of 1\n";
--EXPECT--
start was refused 1 of 1
and it says there is no request here: yes
and it names the member the program wrote: yes
and it is not the store's refusal: yes
Core\Request refuses alike 2 of 2
the process is still readable: draining=false
caught by the root of the tree 1 of 1
